//! Java 1.18+ climate/biome selection, translated to Rust from
//! <https://github.com/niduy/minecraft-world-generator>
//! (`src/lib/minecraft/const.ts` `BIOME_PARAMS` / `BIOME_TREE` and
//! `src/lib/minecraft/minecraft.ts` `genOverworld` / `getNode` /
//! `getDistance`), both of which are themselves ports of Cubitect/cubiomes'
//! `biomenoise.c` / `biomes.c`.
//!
//! The selector is nearest-neighbour over the six climate dimensions
//! `[temperature, humidity, continentalness, erosion, depth, weirdness]`,
//! queried with values scaled by 10000. `BIOME_PARAMS` is a shared table of
//! 139 inclusive `[min, max]` ranges; a `BIOME_TREE` node stores six of those
//! range indices in bytes 0-5 and, in bits 48-63, either a child-node index
//! or a leaf marker (`0xff` in bits 56-63, Java biome id in bits 48-55).
//!
//! The arithmetic below mirrors niduy exactly, including the wrapping u64
//! squaring in `get_distance` (`intToUint64` + `multiply64` in his
//! `math.ts`). The wrapping matters: distances are compared as unsigned
//! integers, and clamping or promoting to wider signed types would change
//! which biome wins on ties and shift biome borders.

